<p>Hail claims in Central Texas rarely end with the first estimate. The adjuster inspects, writes a scope, and the payment arrives, and then the roofer opens up the roof and finds something no one could see from the surface. That is where hail damage claim supplements come in. A supplement is the normal, contract-based way to bring the estimate in line with the real cost of repair. Understanding how supplements work can help San Marcos homeowners and business owners avoid paying out of pocket for covered work.</p>

<p>This guide is written by a <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> who reviews hail estimates for properties across Hays County. It explains what a supplement is, the items most often left out, how to document hidden damage and code items, and how to time and present a request so it gets a fair review.</p>

<h2>What a hail damage claim supplement is</h2>

<p>A supplement is a written request to revise an insurer's estimate on an existing claim. It is not a new claim. It uses the same claim number and date of loss and asks the insurer to account for something the original scope did not. Supplements usually fall into four groups:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Missed items:</strong> work that is plainly necessary to repair the damage but was not written, such as starter strip or ridge cap.</li>
<li><strong>Quantity corrections:</strong> roof area, waste, linear feet of gutter, or number of vents measured short.</li>
<li><strong>Pricing corrections:</strong> line items priced below what it actually costs to do the work in the local market at the time of repair.</li>
<li><strong>Newly discovered damage:</strong> conditions found during tear-off or repair that were hidden at the first inspection.</li>
</ul>

<p>Most insurers write property estimates in Xactimate, a line-item estimating program. A supplement is easiest to evaluate when it follows the same structure, so the adjuster can compare item to item and see exactly what changed and why.</p>

<h2>Why the first hail estimate is often incomplete</h2>

<p>Hail is a common loss in this part of Texas. NOAA's Storm Events Database lists 180 hail reports in Hays County from 1967 through 2026, with the largest numbers in May and April. When a big storm hits, such as the May 9, 2024 event that produced 3-inch hail at San Marcos Academy and 2.5-inch hail near the outlet mall (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181409" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA NCEI event record</a>), adjusters may be inspecting many roofs a day. Under that pressure, estimates tend to capture the obvious items and miss the details. Some reasons:</p>

<ul>
<li>The inspection happened before a contractor measured or opened the roof.</li>
<li>The adjuster used a default template that leaves out accessories and trade-specific steps.</li>
<li>Steep slopes, second-story eaves, or wet conditions limited what could be checked safely.</li>
<li>Interior, fence, or outbuilding damage was not reported until later.</li>
</ul>

<p>None of that means the insurer acted improperly. It means the estimate should be treated as a working document that may need to change as more is known. If you want a deeper method for checking a roof estimate, see our guide on <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof insurance estimate</a>.</p>

<h2>Commonly missed items on hail estimates</h2>

<p>The table below lists items we often see left out or under-measured on hail claims for shingle and metal roofs in the San Marcos area. Not every item applies to every roof, and coverage always depends on the policy and the damage.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Why it gets missed</th><th>What supports it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Starter strip at eaves and rakes</td><td>Left off templates or assumed included in shingles</td><td>Manufacturer installation instructions; photos at eaves</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drip edge</td><td>Existing edge looks undamaged, or none was installed before</td><td>Adopted code requirement; photos; measurements</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hip and ridge cap</td><td>Measured short or priced as cut-up field shingles</td><td>Roof diagram with hip and ridge lengths; product type</td></tr>
<tr><td>Step, counter, and headwall flashing</td><td>Treated as reusable when it cannot be removed and reset without damage</td><td>Close-up photos; contractor explanation of tie-ins at walls and chimneys</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pipe jacks, roof vents, turbines</td><td>Counted short or not inspected for hail dents</td><td>Count and photos of each component</td></tr>
<tr><td>Waste factor</td><td>Default percentage too low for a cut-up roof with many hips and valleys</td><td>Measurement report and roof geometry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steep and high-roof charges</td><td>Pitch or story height not recorded</td><td>Pitch gauge photos; elevation photos</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gutters, downspouts, and screens</td><td>Dents and torn screens overlooked on the walk-around</td><td>Photos with chalk marks or a reference object; linear footage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rooftop and ground HVAC components</td><td>Condenser fin damage not inspected or deferred</td><td>HVAC technician findings and photos</td></tr>
<tr><td>Detach and reset items</td><td>Satellite dishes, solar, or lights on the roof not included</td><td>Photos and a list of roof-mounted equipment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overhead and profit</td><td>Left off when the repair needs several trades coordinated</td><td>Scope showing multiple trades and the need for a general contractor</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/supplement-process-collage.webp" alt="Collage of a roof inspection, phone photos of damage, estimate software, and a handshake" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A supplement ties together field photos, measurements, and a line-item estimate.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Hidden damage found during tear-off</h2>

<p>Some of the most important supplement items cannot be seen until the old roof is off. Common examples include:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Damaged or deteriorated decking</strong> that must be replaced before new shingles can be installed properly.</li>
<li><strong>Additional layers</strong> of shingles under the top layer, which add tear-off and disposal labor.</li>
<li><strong>Underlayment and flashing problems</strong> at valleys, walls, and penetrations.</li>
<li><strong>Water damage to sheathing or framing</strong> where hail fractures let water in.</li>
</ul>

<p>When the crew finds something like this, the most important thing is to <strong>stop and document before covering it up</strong>. Take wide and close photos with a tape measure in view, note the location on a roof diagram, and record the quantity, such as the number of decking sheets. Then notify the insurer promptly in writing. Many insurers want a chance to see the condition or at least review photos before it is repaired, so discuss this with your adjuster and contractor before the job starts. Decking and similar hidden items can be some of the easiest supplements to support, and some of the hardest once they are buried under new shingles.</p>

