<h2>Code items on San Marcos roofs</h2>

<p>Code-related items are a frequent source of confusion. The City of San Marcos has enforced the 2021 International Codes, including the IRC and IBC, since April 1, 2023 (<a href="https://www.sanmarcostx.gov/1289/Codes-Ordinances" target="_blank" rel="noopener">City of San Marcos Codes &amp; Ordinances</a>). Under a local amendment, a roof covering replacement permit is not required unless the new roof covering is heavier than the existing one. Even without a permit, the work is still expected to follow the adopted code and the manufacturer's installation instructions.</p>

<p>That matters for supplements in two ways:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Some items are part of a proper installation.</strong> For example, the 2021 IRC addresses drip edge and underlayment for asphalt shingle roofs. If those items are required to complete the repair correctly, they can be part of the repair scope.</li>
<li><strong>Payment for code upgrades depends on coverage.</strong> When a code requirement adds cost beyond replacing what was there, payment usually depends on <em>ordinance or law coverage</em>, which is often a separate, limited coverage. A code requirement does not automatically mean the insurer pays for it.</li>
</ol>

<p>Kyle, Buda, Wimberley, Dripping Springs, New Braunfels, Lockhart, and Seguin each have their own permit offices and adopted codes, and Hays County does not issue residential building permits in unincorporated areas. Check with the right office for your address. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page and the article on <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-san-marcos/">building codes and insurance repairs</a> go further on this topic.</p>

<h2>Documentation and timing</h2>

<p>A supplement is only as strong as the evidence behind it. A complete request usually includes:</p>

<ul>
<li>A cover letter with the claim number, date of loss, and a short list of what is being added or corrected.</li>
<li>A line-item estimate that tracks the insurer's format, showing each new or changed item.</li>
<li>Dated photos tied to each item, including wide shots for location and close-ups for detail.</li>
<li>Measurements or a measurement report for quantities.</li>
<li>Contractor invoices or bids for items that are hard to price, such as specialty materials.</li>
<li>Code or manufacturer references for items required for proper installation.</li>
</ul>

<p>On timing, sooner is almost always better. Send newly discovered damage as soon as it is found. Send missed-item and pricing supplements once you have reviewed the estimate and gathered support, not after the job is finished and the evidence is gone. Keep an eye on policy deadlines, including any deadline to complete repairs and recover depreciation under a replacement cost policy and any contractual deadline to file suit. Texas Prompt Payment rules in <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Insurance Code Chapter 542</a> also set response timelines once the insurer has the information it requested.</p>

<h2>Communicating with the carrier</h2>

<p>Supplements go more smoothly when they are easy to review. A few habits help:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Keep it organized.</strong> One item per line, each with its own photo and explanation.</li>
<li><strong>Stay factual.</strong> Explain why each item is needed to repair the covered damage. Avoid arguments about motive.</li>
<li><strong>Put it in writing.</strong> Follow any phone call with an email summary, and ask the adjuster to respond to each item, approved or not.</li>
<li><strong>Request a reinspection</strong> when photos alone will not settle a question, such as decking condition or the extent of flashing damage.</li>
<li><strong>Track responses.</strong> Keep a simple log of what was sent, when, and what the insurer said about each item.</li>
</ul>

<p>If the insurer agrees the loss is covered but disagrees about the amount, many policies include an <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> process for resolving that disagreement. If the insurer denies coverage for part of the claim, that is a different question and may call for a review of the policy language or an attorney's advice.</p>

<h2>How a public adjuster can help with supplements</h2>

<p>Preparing a supplement takes time: measuring, photographing, building a line-item estimate, and following up item by item. A public adjuster represents the policyholder, not the insurer or the contractor, and can handle that work on your behalf. Our <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a> service and <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> service cover this work as a San Marcos public adjuster for homes and businesses in San Marcos and nearby Central Texas communities.</p>

<p>Whether you work with a San Marcos public adjuster or handle it yourself, the approach is the same: document thoroughly, request in writing, and keep the deadlines in view. If you would like an independent look at your hail estimate, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> or call [[phone]]. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
